body_text:
A sequel to https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/122001 of sorts.

**Briefly**: When faced with `#![name = #[attr] expr]` (`name` in `crate_name`, `recursion_limit` & `crate_type`) we mostly ignore `#[attr]` but it's more nuanced & slightly differs by attribute.

**Meta note**: While I've marked this issue with https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/labels/F-stmt_expr_attributes which usually implies https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/labels/requires-nightly if the feature is unstable, strictly speaking this bug can be observed on stable toolchains if you use *print requests* like `--print=crate-name` and `--print=file-names` since they don't trigger feature gates.

All subsequent examples assume that the following build step has happened:

`rustc replacer.rs --crate-type=proc-macro --extern proc_macro --edition 2024` where `replacer.rs`:

```rs
use proc_macro::TokenStream;

#[proc_macro_attribute]
pub fn replace_with(arg: TokenStream, _: TokenStream) -> TokenStream { arg }
```

Moreover, they assume that they're placed inside file `file.rs` and get compiled with `rustc file.rs --extern replacer -L. --edition 2024` (plus `--print=crate-name` etc. when it says so).

---

## `#![crate_name = #[attribute] "literal"]`

Under normal execution, the compiler resolves+expands `attribute` & checks that the expansion is a string literal. However, the code responsible for validating & registering the crate name literally only looks at `"literal"` and ignores the presence of `#[attribute]`. It's expected that this part of the code doesn't perform macro expansion (namely, since PR https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/pull/117584). However, **I expected the code to reject any attributes found on the string literal** (this of course includes non-normal executions caused by print requests) to honor lang FCP https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/122001#issuecomment-2023203182.

### Examples

```rs
#![feature(stmt_expr_attributes, proc_macro_hygiene)]
#![crate_name = #[replacer::replace_with("fake")] "real"]
fn main() {}
```

Compiles while it shouldn't. The crate name is actually set to `real` not to `fake`.

```rs
#![crate_name = #[replacer::replace_with("fake")] "real"]
fn main() {}
```

Additionally pass `--print=crate-name` or `--print=file-names`. Compiles while it shouldn't (use of unstable features, attribute on string literal). Outputs `real` for the former & `libreal.rlib` for the latter.

```rs
#![feature(stmt_expr_attributes, proc_macro_hygiene)]
#![crate_name = #[replacer::replace_with("???")] "real"]
fn main() {}
```

Compiles while it shouldn't. The crate name is actually set to `real`.

```rs
#![feature(stmt_expr_attributes, proc_macro_hygiene)]
#![crate_name = #[replacer::replace_with()] "real"]
fn main() {}
```

(Correctly errors with `expected expression, found end of macro arguments`.)

```rs
#![feature(stmt_expr_attributes, proc_macro_hygiene)]
#![crate_name = #[replacer::replace_with(0)] "real"]
fn main() {}
```

(Correctly errors with ``malformed `crate_name` attribute input``.)

## `#![crate_type = #[attribute] "literal"]`

META: TODO: Summary?

### Examples

```rs
#![feature(stmt_expr_attributes, proc_macro_hygiene)]
#![crate_type = #[replacer::replace_with("bin")] "lib"]
```

Compiles while it shouldn't. The set of crate types is actually set to {`lib`} not to {`bin`}.

```rs
#![feature(stmt_expr_attributes, proc_macro_hygiene)]
#![crate_type = #[replacer::replace_with("???")] "lib"]
```

Actually rejects the `???` this with ``invalid `crate_type` value``!

```rs
#![feature(stmt_expr_attributes, proc_macro_hygiene)]
#![crate_type = #[replacer::replace_with("lib")] "???"]
```

Actually ignores both `???` and `lib` & thus fails with `` `main` function not found in crate `file` ``.

```rs
#![crate_type = #[replacer::replace_with("???")] "!!!"]
```

Additionally pass `--print=file-names`. Compiles while it probably shouldn't. Ignores the attribute entirely meaning it doesn't complain about either `???` or `!!!`. Outputs `file` meaning it assumes the default set of crate types, that being {`bin`}.

## `#![recursion_limit = #[attribute] expression]`

