body_text:
I tried this code:


goidbolt:https://godbolt.org/z/9d83b9Ksd


```rust
use std::cmp::Ordering;

#[no_mangle]
#[inline(never)]
pub fn match_loop(values: &[i32], element: i32) -> (usize, usize) {
    let mut left = 0;
    let mut right = values.len() - 1;

    while left < values.len() {
        match element.cmp(&values[right]) {
            Ordering::Equal => return (left + 1, right + 1),
            Ordering::Greater => left += 1,
            Ordering::Less => {
                if right == 0 {
                    break;
                }
                right -= 1;
            }
        }
    }

    (0, 0)
}

#[no_mangle]
#[inline(never)]
pub fn if_loop(values: &[i32], element: i32) -> (usize, usize) {
    let mut left = 0;
    let mut right = values.len() - 1;

    while left < values.len() {
        if element.cmp(&values[right]) == Ordering::Equal {
            return (left + 1, right + 1);
        } else if element.cmp(&values[right]) == Ordering::Greater {
            left += 1;
        } else {
            if right == 0 {
                break;
            }
            right -= 1;
        }
    }

    (0, 0)
}
```



I expected to see this happen: because the `Ordering` value is used only to
choose one of three branches, its integer encoding could be eliminated, leaving
branches on the original comparison flags, as in `if_loop`. This is an
optimization opportunity, not a requirement that equivalent source expressions
always produce identical assembly. Although `if_loop` spells `cmp` twice,
`i32::cmp` is pure here and the emitted code performs only one machine comparison
per loop iteration.

Instead, this happened: `match_loop` first materializes an `Ordering` value and
then branches on it:

```asm
cmp    (%r8,%rdi,4),%ecx
setl   %r10b
setg   %r9b
sub    %r10b,%r9b
cmp    $0x1,%r9b
je     ...
movzbl %r9b,%r9d
cmp    $0xff,%r9d
```

`if_loop` keeps the result in the condition flags:

```asm
cmp    (%rdi,%rdx,4),%ecx
je     ...
jg     ...
```

The symbol-size difference is stable across optimization-for-speed and
optimization-for-size modes:

```text
opt level     match_loop     if_loop
O2                   124          78
O3                   124          81
Os                   106          75
Oz                    94          72
```



### Native timings

Each cell is the median of three runs of 50,000,000 non-inlined calls at O3.
The input slice contains 16 zeros; the search value is 0, 1, or -1 respectively.
Each measurement has 100,000 warm-up calls, and function order alternates
between rounds.

| Fixed input | `match_loop` | `if_loop` |
|---|---:|---:|
| Equal to the last element (1 loop iteration) | 125.394 ms | 124.059 ms |
| Greater than every element (16 Greater iterations) | 664.092 ms | 575.201 ms |
| Less than every element (16 Less iterations) | 855.638 ms | 686.800 ms |

Environment: AMD EPYC 7K62 (guest-reported model), 2 vCPUs, approximately
7.5 GiB RAM, Ubuntu 24.04.4 LTS, Linux 6.8.0-117-generic, KVM VM, pinned to
guest CPU 1. Compiler: nightly-2026-10-01 (`21b707e3f`, LLVM 23.1.1), O3,
codegen-units=1, LTO disabled, overflow checks disabled.



