body_text:
I was trying to get the following code to autovectorize, compiling with `-Copt-level=3 -Ctarget-cpu=x86-64-v4 -Ctarget-feature=+fast-gather`. The to_int and clamp part is not that relevant, but should increase the instruction costs so much that a gather instruction becomes beneficial, compared to a scalarized implementation.

```rust
#[inline(never)]
pub unsafe fn clamp_gather_autovec1(x: &[f64; 8], y: &[f64], out: &mut [f64; 8]) {
    unsafe {
        for i in 0..8 {
            let j = x[i].to_int_unchecked::<usize>().clamp(10, 100);
            out[i] = *y.get_unchecked(j);
        }
    }
}
```

I expected to see this happen: The result should get autovectorized and use gather instructions for accessing the slice.

Instead, this happened: The loop does get unrolled and uses scalar instructions instead.

A discussion with Claude Code pointed to an `assume` instruction inside the `get_unchecked` as the cause of missing autovectorization. And in fact using pointer reads makes it optimize

```rust
#[inline(never)]
pub unsafe fn clamp_gather_autovec2(x: &[f64; 8], y: &[f64], out: &mut [f64; 8]) {
    unsafe {
        for i in 0..8 {
            let j = x[i].to_int_unchecked::<usize>().clamp(10, 100);
            //std::intrinsics::assume(j < y.len());

            out[i] = y.as_ptr().add(j).read();
        }
    }
}
```

https://rust.godbolt.org/z/v5sWW59cc

I'm not sure whether this is expected, and whether it would be considered an issue with rust generating the `assume`, or with llvm not optimizing in the presence of assumes.

Update: Should have switched the `clamp` and `to_int_unchecked`, and first check for NaN to actually satisfy some safety preconditions, but that does not change the autovectorization behavior.

