body_text:
I tried this code:


godbolt:https://godbolt.org/z/ePaK9jrqa



```rust
#[no_mangle]
pub fn direct() -> Vec<u32> {
    let mut n = 42u32;
    let mut out = Vec::new();
    while n > 0 {
        out.push(n % 16);
        n /= 16;
    }
    out.reverse();
    if out.is_empty() {
        out.push(0);
    }
    out
}

#[no_mangle]
pub fn moved() -> Vec<u32> {
    let mut n = 42u32;
    let mut out = Vec::new();
    while n > 0 {
        out.push(n % 16);
        n /= 16;
    }
    let mut copied = out;
    copied.reverse();
    if copied.is_empty() {
        copied.push(0);
    }
    copied
}
```


I expected to see this happen: the constant `42` produces two pushes, constructing
`[10, 2]`, and both functions return `[2, 10]`. Moving the vector to another local
does not clone it or allocate a second buffer. The length remains two, so the
generic reversal loops and the subsequent empty-vector branch could be removed,
as they are in `direct`.

Instead, this happened: at O3 on x86_64, `direct` is 194 bytes and `moved` is
429 bytes.

In optimized LLVM IR, `direct` uses a `<2 x i32>` load, shuffle and store for the
swap. In `moved`, the two-element length is stored before copying the Vec
representation, but a subsequent length load remains. The relevant sequence is
shown below with SSA names and attributes abbreviated:

```llvm
store i64 2, ptr %out_len
call void @llvm.memcpy.p0.p0.i64(ptr %copied, ptr %out, i64 24, i1 false)
%copied_len = getelementptr i8, ptr %copied, i64 16
%len = load i64, ptr %copied_len
; ... generic reversal preheader ...
%small = icmp ult i64 %len, 16
br i1 %small, label %scalar_reverse, label %vector_reverse
```

The optimized IR retains scalar and vectorized reversal loops, including a
vector path for lengths of at least 16 that cannot be needed for this input.
The x86 assembly retains extra branches and the generic reversal code too.
This looks like a lost constant-propagation / simplification opportunity.

Keeping the empty check after `reverse()` matters for this reproducer: removing
it makes this particular size increase disappear in the reduced control.



