```rust
#[derive(Clone, Copy)]
#[allow(dead_code)]
union U {
    t: (u8, u8),
    x: u8,
}

#[inline(never)]
fn demo(x: u8) -> u8 {
    let u = U { x };
    unsafe {
        match u.t {
            (1, 1) => 10,
            (2, 2) => 20,
            _ => 30,
        }
    }
}

fn main() {
    assert_eq!(demo(5), 30);
}
```
When run with Miri, the assertion passes, except if I enable EarlyOtherwiseBranch with `RUSTFLAGS=-Zmir-enable-passes=+EarlyOtherwiseBranch cargo +nightly miri run`, I get:
```
error: Undefined Behavior: reading memory at alloc207[0x1..0x2], but memory is uninitialized at [0x1..0x2], and this operation requires initialized memory
  --> src/main.rs:12:9
   |
12 |         match u.t {
   |         ^^^^^^^^^ Undefined Behavior occurred here
   |
```
When compiled with `-O -Zmir-opt-level=0` the program runs fine, but on my machine, when I add `-Zmir-enable-passes=+EarlyOtherwiseBranch` I get a SIGILL.

I think the relevant part of the MIR diff is this (the full diff is large but this part is instructive):
```diff
         StorageDead(_3);
-        switchInt(copy ((_2.0: (u8, u8)).0: u8)) -> [1: bb2, 2: bb3, otherwise: bb1];
+        _4 = Ne(copy ((_2.0: (u8, u8)).0: u8), copy ((_2.0: (u8, u8)).1: u8));
+        switchInt(move _4) -> [0: bb5, otherwise: bb1];
     }
```
The mess of SwitchInts we get from normal match lowering causes the original MIR to only read the first byte of the union, but EarlyOtherwiseBranch assumes it is okay to read both.

This _feels like_ the same bug as https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/164112, at least in the sense that these MIR passes seem to be unifying arms whose patterns read different bytes of the scrutinee.

