body_text:
The following code compiles:

```rust
pub fn main() {
      let mut a: Vec<u64> = vec![1];
      let mut b: Vec<u64> = vec![2];
      let mut p: &mut Vec<u64> = &mut a;
      let s: &u64;
      // The receiver `&mut *p` is reserved first. The argument then borrows through `p`, and points `p` elsewhere.
      p.push({
          s = &p[0];
          p = &mut b;
          0
      });
      // `a` has been pushed to, so it may have reallocated. `s` still points into its old buffer.
      for _ in 0..64 { p.push(7); }
      println!("{}", *s);
  }
```

I expected to see this happen: compilation fails

Instead, this happened: prints random garbage when run

### Meta

Happens on nightly, stable, and past stables at least back to 1.85.0 on godbolt:

https://rust.godbolt.org/z/zzsTbsKfo

