I think I've hit a odd corner case that is a bit difficult to articulate for me that is largely dependent on emitting `thin-link-bitcode`.

This triggers to run ThinLTOBitcodeWriterPass which is not great and will cause the generated `.o` and `.indexing.o` (and potentially `.bc` if also emitting that) to have a different module hash since they are generated by other ThinLTOBitcodeWriterPass invocations. This can have a range if weird side affects (particularly with CFI enabled that sets `EnableSplitLTOUnit`). E.g. with CFI this strips debug info from `.o` and an upcoming LLVM change (merged in `main` llvm-project but not in the version used by Rust: https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/commit/aa91871539ca518e81af58485df895f8db30496a), this causes a second `ExternalLinkage` global variable created in the `.o` that is not present in the `.indexing.o` file.

From tracing the code this seems to be the case because the `.indexing.o` is generated and emitted from `optimize()` (since https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/pull/133250) but the generated `.o` is 'ignored' and then regenerated by `codegen()`.

Now I'd love to get some input from experts on this part of the code on what the best way to solve this is.

I see two ways, 1) change the code back to emit `.o`, `.indexing.o` and `.bc` all at the same state from the same `ThinLTOBitcodeWriterPass` invocation. The change basically moving away from this suggests this is not intended or 2) Reuse the already generated `.o` file from running `optimize()` if it exists when running `codegen()` avoiding the second run of ThinLTOBitcodeWriterPass there. This looks like it's in line with how embedded bitcode is extracted from `module.thin_lto_buffer`.

cc'ing some folks who might be interested or touched this code before: @dianqk, @bjorn3, @rcvalle 

