I tried this code:

```rust
pub fn f(items: &[u64]) -> u64 {
    let mut acc = 0;
    for (i, it) in items.iter().enumerate() {
        for j in (1..i.min(8)).step_by(2) {
            acc = j as u64;
        }
        acc += it;
    }
    acc
}
```
```sh
rustc --edition 2024 --crate-type=lib -C opt-level=3 a.rs
```

I expected to see this happen: it compiles.

Instead, this happened: with LLVM assertions enabled, LLVM's loop peeling hits an assertion.

```
rustc: /checkout/src/llvm-project/llvm/lib/Transforms/Utils/LoopPeel.cpp:1114: void llvm::peelLoop(Loop *, unsigned int, bool, LoopInfo *, ScalarEvolution *, DominatorTree &, AssumptionCache *, bool, ValueToValueMapTy &): Assertion `(!PeelLast || (canPeelLastIteration(*L, *SE) && PeelCount == 1)) && "when peeling the last iteration, the loop must be supported and can " "only peel a single iteration"' failed.
```

The same happens at `-C opt-level=2` and with `-C codegen-units=1`. LLVM issue: llvm/llvm-project#231037. It still asserts on LLVM main.

Release toolchains have no LLVM assertions, and they compile this short function. On larger code, though, the same bug crashes them: the program below makes stable 1.99, 1.98 and nightly crash with SIGSEGV in `LoopFullUnrollPass`.

