I tried this code:

```rust
fn main() {
    dbg!(size_of_val(&bar()));
    assert!(size_of_val(&bar()) < 40_000);
}
async fn bar() {
    // if brackets commented size: 40003 else 2
    // {
    let res: Result<(), [u8; 40000]> = Ok(());
    assert!(res.is_ok());
    // }
    foo().await;
}
async fn foo() {}
```
I expected to see the size of the future generated by bar to be small.

Instead, I observed it bigger than 40000. By adding a group to destroy the `res` variable before the await point I got the expected behavior with a size of 2. I expected the compiler to be smart enough to detect that res is not used after the await point and avoid this useless copy. 

### Meta

`rustc --version --verbose`:
```
rustc 1.101.0-nightly (e9bbadf68 2026-10-12)
```
It can be reproduced with older versions of the compiler.

