Thank you for filing a rustdoc issue! Rustdoc is the tool that handles the generation of docs.  It is usually invoked via `cargo doc`, but can also be used directly.

If you have an issue with the actual content of the docs, use the "Documentation problem" template instead.
-->

Essentially, we'd like to avoid the confusion that came when creating [ACP 916](https://github.com/rust-lang/libs-team/issues/916).

Some types, like `Box`, are allowed to directly implement functions on their receiver:

```rust
// note: implemented on [T], not Box<[T], A>!
impl<T, A: Allocator> [T] {
    pub fn into_vec(self: Box<[T], A>) -> Vec<T, A> { /* ... */ }
}
```

In all cases, this makes sense to include on the page for the receiver: in other words, here, it is shown on the page for `slice`. If this were implemented instead for some user-defined crate, e.g. like:

```rust
// note: implemented on MyType, not Box<MyType>!
impl MyType {
    pub fn unbox(self: Box<Self>) -> Self { /* ... */ }
}
```

Then it would make no sense to put this on the `Box` page because, well, it's not part of the standard library.

*However*, the standard library is uniquely positioned to be able to put these methods directly on `Box` instead. For example:

```rust
// note: implemented on Box<MaybeUninit<T>, A>, not MaybeUninit<T>!
impl<T, A: Allocator> Box<MaybeUninit<T>, A>
    pub unsafe fn assume_init(self) -> Box<T, A> { /* ... */ }
}
```

If `MaybeUninit<T>` were defined in a separate crate, it would need to be an inherent method on `MaybeUninit`, but since it's defined in the standard library, we can put it on `Box` instead.

In general, the standard library docs are special when it comes to arbitrary self types: if both the receiver and the smart pointer are standard library types, then a relevant method should be included on *both pages* since it is relevant in both places. Additionally, we should probably allow the "wrong" syntax for searches, e.g. searching `Box::into_vec` or `MaybeUninit::assume_init` should point to `slice::into_vec` and `Box::assume_init`.
