Live Reproducer: https://play.rust-lang.org/?version=stable&mode=debug&edition=2021&gist=4c75f7c599fad96a4ea6f44ec5b37524

Running the following code shows that `nth(0)` and `next()` of the same fresh iterator return different values. `nth(0)` should return what `next()` returns.

```rust
/// A legal non-fused iterator: `None` once, then `Some(1)`, `Some(2)`, ...
struct Gap(u32);

impl Iterator for Gap {
    type Item = u32;
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<u32> {
        self.0 += 1;
        if self.0 == 1 { None } else { Some(self.0 - 1) }
    }
}

fn main() {
    let next = Gap(0).skip(1).chain(std::iter::empty()).next();
    let nth0 = Gap(0).skip(1).chain(std::iter::empty()).nth(0);
    println!("{:?} {:?}", next, nth0);
}
```

Output (wrong):

    None Some(2)

Expected:

    None None

Notes:
- Only non-fused iterators are affected.
- Without the `chain`, `nth(0)` and `next()` agree.
- Present since 1.57.

rustc version:

    rustc 1.99.1 (58cf8f9c3 2026-10-13)

This reproduces with the current nightly (rustc 1.101.0-nightly (4ebd25825 2026-10-15)).

