body_text:
cc: @ZuseZ4 

We are currently looking into LLM inference workloads on `std::offload`, where splitting attention over the context needs a float atomic add. Empirically in my local benchmark (RX 9070 XT, gfx1201), splitting attention this way increases the performance of GPT-2 decoding from 171 token/s to 245 token/s at 1,023 tokens when the partial sums are combined with the native float atomic add via inline assembly. With the suggested CAS-loop workaround, it still reaches 233 tokens/s, so the native add itself contributes ~5%.

Past issues #68614 suggested using `to_bits` and `AtomicU64`, and was closed partly because "llvm doesn't currently have any intrinsics for f32, f64", but now that LLVM has `atomicrmw fadd`, #72353 was closed "unless there is a compelling use case for exposing float atomic support" and `std::offload` is now that GPU usecase.

Additionally, the workaround suggested in https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/72353#issuecomment-1093729062 only has a load and a store. Used as `store(load() + x)`, it loses updates, so a real add still needs a compare-exchange retry loop, which is expensive on the GPU.

My local experiments show that Rust matches C++ (HIP's `atomicAdd`) performance only with inline assembly. Without assembly, Rust is ~3.8x slower on the attention kernel at 1024 context lengths, more than 300x slower on one contended float, 3-5x slower on a tuned vocabulary softmax sum, and up to 400x slower with a one thread per element launch.

### Experiement code.

<details>
<summary>Rust (plain nightly, no offload needed)</summary>

```rust
#![no_std]
#![feature(abi_gpu_kernel, asm_experimental_arch, link_llvm_intrinsics)]
#![allow(internal_features)]
use core::sync::atomic::{AtomicU32, Ordering::Relaxed};

#[panic_handler]
fn panic(_: &core::panic::PanicInfo) -> ! { loop {} }

// #72353's suggested workaround: the float's bits in an AtomicU32 + a compare_exchange retry loop
fn add_cas(p: *mut f32, x: f32) {
    let a = unsafe { AtomicU32::from_ptr(p.cast()) };
    let mut cur = a.load(Relaxed);
    while let Err(seen) = a.compare_exchange_weak(cur, (f32::from_bits(cur) + x).to_bits(), Relaxed, Relaxed) {
        cur = seen;
    }
}

// native instruction via inline asm
fn add_asm(p: *mut f32, x: f32) {
    unsafe {
        core::arch::asm!("global_atomic_add_f32 {off}, {v}, {base} scope:SCOPE_DEV",
            off = in(vgpr32) 0u32, v = in(vgpr32) x, base = in(sgpr64) p as u64);
    }
}

unsafe extern "llvm-intrinsic" {
    #[link_name = "llvm.amdgcn.flat.atomic.fadd.f32.p0"]
    fn flat_atomic_fadd(p: *mut f32, v: f32, ordering: i32, scope: i32, volatile: bool) -> f32;
}

// every lane adds 1.0 into p[0]
#[unsafe(no_mangle)]
pub extern "gpu-kernel" fn add_cas_kernel(p: *mut f32) { add_cas(p, 1.0) }
#[unsafe(no_mangle)]
pub extern "gpu-kernel" fn add_asm_kernel(p: *mut f32) { add_asm(p, 1.0) }
#[unsafe(no_mangle)]
pub extern "gpu-kernel" fn add_intrinsic_kernel(p: *mut f32) { unsafe { flat_atomic_fadd(p, 1.0, 2, 0, false); } }
```

Crate type `cdylib`, built with:
`RUSTFLAGS="-Ctarget-cpu=gfx1201" cargo +nightly rustc -r --target amdgcn-amd-amdhsa -Zbuild-std=core -- --emit=asm`
</details>

<details>
<summary>C++ (HIP)</summary>

```cpp
#include <hip/hip_runtime.h>

// every lane adds 1.0 into p[0]
__global__ void add_atomic_kernel(float* p) { atomicAdd(p, 1.0f); }
__global__ void add_unsafe_kernel(float* p) { unsafeAtomicAdd(p, 1.0f); }
```

Built with: `hipcc -O3 --offload-arch=gfx1201 --cuda-device-only -S`
</details>

