body_text:
Applying the `export_name` attribute on a required trait associated function (a function item without a body that's inside a trait definition) is disallowed by the compiler. However, doing so on a provided trait associated function (a function with a body that's inside a trait definition) is allowed. It's unclear how it is supposed to be expected to behave.

For example, the following fails with a linker error: 

```rust
pub trait Trait {
    #[unsafe(export_name = "lol")]
    extern "C" fn wut() {}
}

pub fn main() {
    unsafe extern "C" {
        safe fn lol();
    }

    lol();
}
```

Implementing the trait on a type still results in a linker error... unless we call the associated function. The following code compiles:

```rust
pub trait Trait {
    #[unsafe(export_name = "lol")]
    extern "C" fn wut() {}
}

pub fn main() {
    unsafe extern "C" {
        safe fn lol();
    }

    lol();

    struct Thing;
    impl Trait for Thing {}

    Thing::wut();
}
```

However, the above code has a linker error if we compile it in release mode.

If we implement the trait on two types, and call both associated functions, we again have an error. (In debug mode, we have what appears to be a compiler error that isn't a linker error, due to conflicting symbols. In release mode, we have a linker error due to a missing symbol):

```rust
pub trait Trait {
    #[unsafe(export_name = "lol")]
    extern "C" fn wut() {}
}

pub fn main() {
    unsafe extern "C" {
        safe fn lol();
    }

    lol();

    struct Thing;
    impl Trait for Thing {}

    Thing::wut();

    struct Thing2;
    impl Trait for Thing2 {}

    Thing2::wut();
}
```

Casting a value to a trait object doesn't count as a "use" of the trait method, probably because of mir optimizations that are enabled even in debug mode:

```rust
pub trait Trait {
    #[unsafe(export_name = "lol")]
    extern "C" fn wut(&self) {}
}

pub fn main() {
    unsafe extern "C" {
        safe fn lol(_: *const ());
    }

    lol(1 as _);

    struct Thing;
    impl Trait for Thing {}

    let _ = &Thing as &dyn Trait;
}
```

Actually putting the value into a variable makes it compile again (this time it compiles both in debug and release mode, don't ask me why):

```rust
pub trait Trait {
    #[unsafe(export_name = "lol")]
    extern "C" fn wut(&self) {}
}

pub fn main() {
    unsafe extern "C" {
        safe fn lol(_: *const ());
    }

    lol(1 as _);

    struct Thing;
    impl Trait for Thing {}

    let _x = &Thing as &dyn Trait;
}
```

I don't know how this is supposed to work, but this set of behaviours is, to me, clearly inconsistent and broken.

### Meta

Reproducible on the playground with version `1.99.1`.

