body_text:
I have been building a big test suite (on top of Bevy) on my Linux and Windows CI runners.
On Windows I have repeatedly run into "memory allocation failed" and os error 1455.
Increasing the page file size from 4GB to 32 GB and then to 96 GB worked around it, although the page file itself stays almost unused.
On Linux I encountered no such issues.

I investigated this with an AI assistant (Claude Opus 5.5 in Claude Code). 

Everything below was written by it, since I am unfortunately not familiar enough with the subject to make any meaningful contribution, or provide a better explanation of what is going on.
I ran the repro myself on my own machine.

> **AI-written explanation (Claude Opus 5.5):**
>
> Since Rust 1.78, rustc maps the files of the crates it depends on (`.rlib`, `.rmeta`) into memory as copy-on-write: #122309 switched `rustc_data_structures::memmap::Mmap::map` to memmap2's `map_copy_read_only`. On Windows, a copy-on-write mapping lets the process change a private copy of any page, so Windows reserves memory (commit charge) for the whole file up front, even though rustc only reads it. Commit counts against a fixed limit of RAM plus page file, and allocations fail once it is reached, however much RAM is free.
>
> So each rustc commits about the total size of the dependency files it reads. With one rustc per thread in a large workspace, that reaches the limit. The repro below shows rustc 1.78 and later committing the size of a 1 to 2 GiB rlib with a working set under 40 MiB, and 1.77.2 committing 8 MiB for the same work.
>
> A plain read-only mapping (`memmap2::Mmap::map`) commits nothing on Windows, so using it there should fix this. The virtiofs problem behind #122309 only exists on Linux, and `Mmap` only hands out `&[u8]`, so nothing relies on being able to write.

### Code

I tried this code (run it in an empty folder: `rustc repro.rs && repro.exe`):

```rust
