<details><summary>Generator for N components (each depends on the previous two)</summary>

```python
import sys
n, deps = int(sys.argv[1]), int(sys.argv[2])
print("""pub trait Module {}
pub trait HasComponent<I: ?Sized> {}
pub trait Component<M> {
    type Interface: ?Sized;
}

pub struct Container;
impl Module for Container {}
""")
for i in range(n):
    bounds = "".join(f" + HasComponent<dyn T{i-d}>" for d in range(1, deps + 1) if i - d >= 0)
    print(f"pub trait T{i} {{}}")
    print(f"pub struct C{i};")
    print(f"impl<M: Module{bounds}> Component<M> for C{i} {{ type Interface = dyn T{i}; }}")
    print(f"impl HasComponent<<C{i} as Component<Container>>::Interface> for Container {{}}")
```

</details>

This is the impl shape that [`shaku`](https://crates.io/crates/shaku) 0.6.3 (a DI crate) generates. `#[derive(Component)]` emits `impl<M: Module + HasComponent<dyn Dep>…> Component<M> for X`. `module!` emits one `impl HasComponent<<X as Component<Self>>::Interface> for Module` per component. Real applications have hundreds of components.

I expected this to happen: the crate type-checks in well under a second, as on 1.98.1.

Instead, this happened: coherence time grows exponentially with the number of components (about 3× per added component):

| components (2 deps each) | 1.97.1 | 1.99.1 |
|---|---|---|
| 8  | 0.3 s | 3.3 s |
| 10 | 0.1 s | 31.5 s |
| 12 | 0.3 s | > 120 s (killed) |

