body_text:
### What it does

This lint disallows `pub use` of items that do not belong to submodules of the current module.

Optionally it could also disallow `pub use` of items that do belong to public submodules of the current module.



### Advantage

* This ensures that by default, every entity not owned by the current module has an unique "canonical" import path.
* In large multi-crate workspaces, because this rule will enforce importing entities from where they are actually defined: this will help avoid redundant dependencies and tighter coupling between crates that shouldn't depend on each other. Otherwise, in real-life codebase it's not rare to see crate A depending on crate B just for reexport of something from crate C on which crate A already depends.

### Drawbacks

* During refactorings which move the code around, there will be extra friction against just setting up `pub use` reexports at old paths; one would either have to `#[expect(...)]` this new warning on reexports (or not have the warning enabled in the first place), or have to update all imports as well (impossible for things that are part of API of public crates without a major version bump, and tedious even for private crates of private multi-crate workspaces). On the other hand, if, in a large multi-crate workspace, some functionality has been extracted from a large crate A into a smaller more independent crate B, there is value in updating all imports of this functionality in other crates from A to B, even though it's tedious.
* Sometimes it makes sense to reexport foreign imports, e.g. to provide self-contained macros defined via `macro_rules`, or to encapsulate implementation details and foreign dependencies from callers (not applicable to structs and enums because these can be reexported using type aliases).

### Example

```rust
// crate-a/src/a.rs
pub use super::SomethingX;
pub use crate::y::SomethingY;
pub use ::other_crate::SomethingZ;

// crate-b/src/lib.rs
use ::crate_a::a::SomethingX;
use ::crate_a::a::SomethingY;
use ::crate_a::a::SomethingZ;
```

Could be written as:

```rust
// crate-a/src/a.rs
use super::SomethingX;
use crate::y::SomethingY;
use ::other_crate::SomethingZ;

// crate-b/src/lib.rs
use ::crate_a::SomethingX;
use ::crate_a::y::SomethingY;
use ::other_crate::SomethingZ;
```

On the other hand, the following code would be allowed:
```
mod inner_private {
    pub struct Something;
}

pub use inner_private::Something;
```

The following code could also optionally be allowed (also see below for comparison with the existing `pub_use` lint):
```
pub mod inner_public {
    pub struct Something;
}

pub use inner_public::Something;
```

### Comparison with existing lints

Unlike `clippy::pub_use`, this lint has the opposite goal (although their functionality overlaps).

While examples from documentation for `clippy::pub_use` cover reexports of items from submodules of the current module, on large codebases this might be an useful expression, simplifying code organization, where each outer module acting as encapsulation boundary, explicitly declaring its API, instead of callers having access to its internals, or instead of having to define all exported items in the outer module, in the same file. With this approach, a module (which might also be the main module of a crate) will typically have multiple submodules, none of them public, and reexport a limited set of items from these submodules as its public API. In this scenario, `clippy::pub_use` cannot be used because legitimate usages of `pub use` are the norm, widespread across the codebase.

However, there is still value in preventing by default accidental (or not so accidental) reexports of items the reexporting code doesn't own. Right now there is no way to achieve this without also banning reexport of the items the reexporting code does own.

### Additional Context

This suggestion is motivated by a specific pain point with the specific existing large multi-crate codebase where crates, for historical reasons, are very tightly coupled together, with too many dependencies going between crates (putting significant constraints on how `cargo build` / `cargo clippy` can build crates in parallel), even though a lot of these dependencies are not actually needed. And with plenty of "legitimate" `pub use` statements (as described above), it is implausible to separate them from "unwanted" `pub use` statements, without spending a lot of cognitive effort on each of the thousands of `pub use` statements (and even if it was possible, the clean state wouldn't last for long).

