body_text:
### What it does

Warns when an inner attribute (`#![attr]`) is immediately followed by a non-empty line without a blank line in between.

This lint would be part of the `suspicious` category.

### Advantage

* Improves readability by visually separating attributes from the code they apply to. Without the blank line, an inner attribute can look like it was intended to be an outer attribute (`#[attr]`). If it is intended to be an inner attribute, a blank line should separate it from the following code.

### Drawbacks

* Primarily a formatting/style issue with no effect on correctness.

### Example

```rust
#![allow(unused_variables)]
fn foo() {
    let x = 1;
}

fn bar() {
    let x = 1;
}
```

Could be written as:

```rust
#![allow(unused_variables)]

fn foo() {
    let x = 1;
}

fn bar() {
    let x = 1;
}
```


### Comparison with existing lints

The closest existing lint is ⁠[`empty_line_after_outer_attr`](https://rust-lang.github.io/rust-clippy/master/#empty_line_after_outer_attr), which checks for an empty line after an outer attribute. This lint would provide the corresponding behavior for inner attributes.

### Additional Context

* rustfmt’s unstable [`blank_lines_lower_bound`](https://rust-lang.github.io/rustfmt/?search=&version=v1.10.0#blank_lines_lower_bound) does not provide a way to enforce a blank line specifically after an inner attribute.

