body_text:
Seemingly straightforward code has unexpected lifetimes elided leading to unsound implementation:
```rust
#[repr(transparent)]
pub struct Wrapper(pub [u8; 4]);

// Elided lifetimes in the impl header are independent:
// `impl<'a, 'b> From<&'a Wrapper> for &'b [u8; 4]`
impl From<&Wrapper> for &[u8; 4] {
    fn from(value: &Wrapper) -> Self {
        // `&*core::ptr::from_ref(value).cast::<[u8; 4]>()` behaves the same
        unsafe { core::mem::transmute(value) }
    }
}

fn dangling() -> &'static [u8; 4] {
    let wrapper = Wrapper([1, 2, 3, 4]);
    <&[u8; 4]>::from(&wrapper)
}

fn main() {
    println!("{:?}", dangling());
}
```

https://play.rust-lang.org/?version=stable&mode=debug&edition=2024&gist=3b4980dc68da1d5b555002705c7f9332


Using safe APIs (possible in this case, but not in general) shows why:
```
error: lifetime may not live long enough
 --> src/main.rs:8:9
  |
7 |     fn from(value: &Wrapper) -> Self {
  |                    -            ---- return type is &'2 [u8; 4]
  |                    |
  |                    let's call the lifetime of this reference `'1`
8 |         &value.0
  |         ^^^^^^^^ associated function was supposed to return data with lifetime `'2` but it is returning data with lifetime `'1`
```

Impl should be written like this to be explicit about it since this is most likely what user intends anyway:

```rust
impl<'a From<&'a Wrapper> for &'a [u8; 4] {
    fn from(value: &'a Wrapper) -> Self {
        unsafe { core::mem::transmute(value) }
    }
}
```

I found this to be a particularly sneaky pitfall, hence lint request.

