### Summary

There are a bunch if iterators where using `for_each` removes branching and improves performance: `x..=y` ranges, `VecDeque` iterator, `.chain()`, etc.
However, `needless_for_each` still triggers there, even though it leads to slower code.

The reproducer example is a little convoluted, since lint is a bit tricky to trigger; for simpler example with asm: [ `(0..=n)` godbolt](https://godbolt.org/#g:!((g:!((g:!((h:codeEditor,i:(filename:'1',fontScale:14,fontUsePx:'0',j:1,lang:rust,selection:(endColumn:2,endLineNumber:16,positionColumn:2,positionLineNumber:16,selectionStartColumn:2,selectionStartLineNumber:16,startColumn:2,startLineNumber:16),source:'%23%5Bunsafe(no_mangle)%5D%0Apub+fn+branching(n:+i32)+-%3E+i32+%7B%0A++++let+mut+res+%3D+0%3B%0A++++for+x+in+0..%3Dn+%7B%0A++++++++res+%2B%3D+x%3B%0A++++%7D%0A++++res%0A%7D%0A%23%5Bunsafe(no_mangle)%5D%0Apub+fn+branchless(n:+i32)+-%3E+i32+%7B%0A++++let+mut+res+%3D+0%3B%0A++++(0..%3Dn).for_each(%7Cx%7C%7B%0A++++++++res+%2B%3D+x%3B%0A++++%7D)%3B%0A++++res%0A%7D'),l:'5',n:'0',o:'Rust+source+%231',t:'0')),k:50,l:'4',n:'0',o:'',s:0,t:'0'),(g:!((h:compiler,i:(compiler:r1990,filters:(b:'0',binary:'1',binaryObject:'1',commentOnly:'0',debugCalls:'1',demangle:'0',directives:'0',execute:'1',intel:'0',libraryCode:'0',trim:'1',verboseDemangling:'0'),flagsViewOpen:'1',fontScale:14,fontUsePx:'0',j:1,lang:rust,libs:!(),options:'-O',overrides:!((name:edition,value:'2024')),selection:(endColumn:1,endLineNumber:1,positionColumn:1,positionLineNumber:1,selectionStartColumn:1,selectionStartLineNumber:1,startColumn:1,startLineNumber:1),source:1),l:'5',n:'0',o:'+rustc+1.99.0+(Editor+%231)',t:'0')),k:50,l:'4',n:'0',o:'',s:0,t:'0')),l:'2',n:'0',o:'',t:'0')),version:4).

P.S. If anything, I think the inverse rule should be true: `for` loops without control flow should be suggested to be rewritten as `for_each`.

### Lint Name

needless_for_each

### Reproducer

I tried this code:

```rust
pub fn bla(n: i32) {
    let mut v: std::collections::VecDeque<i32> = (0..=n).collect();
    if let Some(x) = v.pop_front() {
        v.push_back(x);
    }
    v.into_iter().for_each(|i| {
        println!("{i}");
    });
}
```

