body_text:
### What it does

A common demonstration of something Rust does not guarantee by itself is semantics of arguments, and how you could f.e. misuse one ID for a different one, especially if a refactor swaps arguments while your callers have not adjusted. That would be a thing that is not automatically detected as a breaking change, since the function signature would remain the same.

Branded types are one common solution in these circumstances. The idea is for a lint to prevent that scenario in the first place by warning if a function definition (including in trait definitions, but not in trait implementations) is accepting two or more arguments of the same type, and suggest definition of branded types as a solution.

### Advantage

- Prevent unintended breaking changes to callers of functions.
- Encourage use of branded types for more easily verifiable program semantics.
- Encourage library authors to think about not only the types, but the semantics of their function's arguments.

### Drawbacks

- The lint is not auto fixable, since it would otherwise introduce new symbols, which in turn means different behavior. Still, it would be great for diagnostics.
- It will not capture misuse of different units with the same one raw type, when there's only one of said type. A function taking a single integer for a temperature may still be accepting Celsius, Fahrenheit or some other unit, and produce incorrect results if misused.
- It will report false positives on functions that legitimately need multiple arguments of the same type. Users of this lint will need to opt such functions out, or refactor them in potentially less ergonomic ways. Examples of such functions are things like a sum or concat function, though then again, operations on arrays or vectors may be better suited substitutes anyway, and this lint would encourage library authors to use that. It may be possible to catch such false positives by allowing number suffixes, e.g. "concat(s1: String, s2: String)", though more complex cases like "someOperationOnPoints(x1: u8, y1: u8, x2: u8, y2: u8)" would suggest only a single non-repeating sequence in the names could be auto opted out.
- A branded type may still expose raw access to its internals, which still enables misuse. This lint will not guard against that, though one has to go out of their way to do that (or use a silly AI).

### Example

```rust
impl Preferences {
  fn set(userId: String, productId: String) {
    // ...
  }
}
```

Could be written as:

```rust
struct UserId(String);
struct ProductId(String);

impl Preferences {
  fn set(userId: UserId, productId: ProductId) {
    let userId = userId.0;
    let productId = productId.0;
    // ...
  }
}
```


### Comparison with existing lints

I am not aware of an existing lint that would check for a similar thing, let alone suggest or do the refactor, and I searched among all existing lints that mention the word "type".

### Additional Context

Inspired for this after reading https://smallcultfollowing.com/babysteps/blog/2026/10/14/agentic-dev-and-rust/ and seeing the slides mentioned there, though that is not the first time I've seen the above code example mentioned as something that falls outside of the general "if it compiles, it's probably correct" mentality of non-god tier Rust devs.

